.TH FAIL2ZIG 1 "2026-10-21" "fail2zig 0.4.8" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
fail2zig \- intrusion prevention daemon and its administration commands
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fail2zig
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
[\fB\-\-validate\-config\fR]
[\fB\-\-import\-config\fR [\fIsource\-dir\fR]]
[\fB\-\-import\-output\fR \fIpath\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig
[\fIglobal flags\fR]
\fIcommand\fR
[\fIargs\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig rule-test
(\fB\-\-file\fR \fIpath\fR | \fB\-\-record\fR \fIline\fR | \fB\-\-journal\fR [\fImatch\fR...])
(\fB\-\-service\fR \fIname\fR | \fB\-\-rule\-file\fR \fIpath\fR)
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig migrate
\fBinspect\fR|\fBsnapshot\fR|\fBplan\fR|\fBvalidate\fR|\fBcutover\fR|\fBstatus\fR|\fBrollback\fR
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig repair-source
\fB\-\-jail\fR \fIname\fR \fB\-\-source\fR \fIpath\fR \fB\-\-token\fR \fItoken\fR
\fB\-\-acknowledge\-truncation\fR
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig support-bundle
[\fB\-\-out\fR \fIpath\fR|\fB\-\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-socket\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIms\fR]
[\fB\-\-include\-addresses\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fail2zig
is a host-based intrusion prevention daemon. It reads log files or the systemd
journal, matches compiled service filters or bounded native rules, and applies
temporary bans through nftables (direct netlink), ipset or iptables.
.PP
One executable provides every entry point. Without a command it starts the daemon;
with a command it administers a running daemon over its Unix socket;
.B rule-test
works offline. Migration preparation is offline; cutover, status and rollback form a journaled workflow whose
activation and release steps use the daemon socket. Follow the migration guide for
the required stop/start sequence and safety checks.
.PP
The daemon runs in the foreground only and is designed for a
.BR systemd (1)
unit of
.BR Type=notify :
it sends
.B READY=1
only after configuration, storage, every source that can resume and enforcing protection are admitted,
.B RELOADING=1
then
.B READY=1
around a live reload, and
.B STOPPING=1
on exit.
.B SIGHUP
applies a validated configuration generation live;
.B SIGUSR1
reopens
.B global.log_target
after rotation;
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
stop cleanly. A stop leaves the daemon's realized firewall rules in the kernel, where
they keep their timeouts; the next start reconciles them with durable state. To stop with
an empty table, run
.B fail2zig firewall withdraw
first. No socket unit is shipped and
.B LISTEN_FDS
is not read.
.PP
A source that cannot resume does not hold back
.BR READY=1 :
the daemon pauses that source, restores bans for every jail, enforces every other
enabled jail, sends
.B READY=1
and keeps running, so
.B systemctl is-active fail2zig
reports
.BR active .
Protection stays degraded while a source is paused; see STATUS and REPAIR-SOURCE.
Configuration, storage, firewall and schema refusals still stop the start with exit 1.
.
.SH DAEMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Configuration file. Default:
.IR /etc/fail2zig/config.toml .
Unknown keys are rejected with line and column context.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Run in the foreground. Accepted for clarity; it is the only mode.
.TP
.B \-\-validate\-config
Load and validate the configuration, print the result and exit without starting
sources, touching the firewall or writing state.
.B \-\-test\-config
is an alias.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-config " [source-dir]"
Translate a fail2ban configuration tree (default
.IR /etc/fail2ban )
into a native configuration and a protected provenance manifest, then exit.
Unsupported semantics are marked
.B compatibility_pending
rather than approximated.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-output " path"
Destination of the imported configuration. Default:
.IR /etc/fail2zig/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit without contacting the daemon.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS (ADMINISTRATION)
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Unix socket. Default: the
.B socket_path
set in
.IR /etc/fail2zig/config.toml ,
else
.IR /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-output " format"
.BR table " (default), " json " or " plain
(tab-separated).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI colour.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " ms"
Command timeout. Default 5000. Expiry exits with class 3.
.
.SH COMMANDS
Members of the
.B fail2zig
group may read aggregate monitoring and redacted configuration through the 0660 socket.
Ban lists, confirmed history and firewall inventory require uid 0 or the daemon uid.
Mutations
.RB ( ban ", " unban ", " "ignore add" ", " "ignore remove" ", " reload ", " "history reset" ", " jail )
require uid 0 or the daemon uid, verified with
.BR SO_PEERCRED .
The read-only commands include
.BR "ignore list" .
.PP
Every mutation request, including the online form of
.BR repair-source ,
writes exactly one
.B info
line with scope
.B audit
to the daemon log once its outcome is known, for example:
.PP
.nf
audit: command=ban target=203.0.113.4 jail=sshd uid=0 outcome=applied
.fi
.PP
.B command
is the client verb
.RB ( ban ", " unban ", " "ignore add" ", " "ignore remove" ", " reload ,
.BR "jail pause" ", " "jail resume" ", " "jail enable" ", " "jail disable" ,
.BR "history reset" " or " repair-source ),
.B target
is the address, jail or path, or
.B \-
when there is none (as for
.BR reload ),
.B uid
is the caller's uid from the socket credentials, and
.B outcome
is the daemon's outcome
.RB ( applied ", " rejected ", " absent ", " partial ", " uncertain ", " noop
or
.BR restart_required ).
With
.B log_format = \(dqjson\(dq
the same keys are fields of the line's JSON object.
.TP
.B status
Daemon summary: version, protection state
.RB ( active ", " log-only ", " unknown ", " mixed " or " "DEGRADED (cause)" ),
backend, active bans, retained history confirmations, storage phase, generation and worker/clock flags.
Active bans are what the kernel inventory holds: on nftables the last audit kept current
by the kernel's acknowledgements and change events, on ipset and iptables the last
complete readback. The
.B knowledge
field reports whether that inventory is fresh (the last audit within the verification
cadence and, on nftables, the event stream healthy), stale or absent, with its wall time
and age; stale knowledge keeps the last count and
reports degraded protection, and no readback reports the count as absent rather than
zero. Pending bans, overdue removals and their oldest ages describe work the daemon
has not yet confirmed; a served backlog alone does not degrade protection.
Diagnostic rows show known storage, source and firewall causes. A missing cause is
reported as unknown; it does not establish healthy protection. The recovery retry
deadline is in absolute monotonic milliseconds, not a remaining delay.
Plain output adds named diagnostic keys; JSON fields are additive and optional.
Table output uses a compact protection summary. Add
.B --details
to include identifiers and additional counters; failures remain visible without it.
History counts retained confirmed effect events; maintenance can remove these rows,
so this is not a lifetime ban count. The machine-readable key remains
.BR total_bans .
.B memory_admission_bytes
is the memory the daemon planned and admitted against
.BR native_memory_ceiling_mb ;
it grows when a reload adds a jail and shrinks when a reload removes one,
and it is not the resident size.
Plain output prints it after
.BR paused_sources ,
and
.B --details
shows it as
.BR "Memory plan:" .
.RS
.PP
State file and tracking pool. Table output adds the lines
.PP
.nf
State file: <bytes> (<pages> pages of <max_pages>)
Tracking pool: <tracked> of <tracking_limit> tracked, <banned> of <ban_limit> banned
.fi
.PP
and JSON adds
.BR state_file_bytes ,
.BR state_file_pages ,
.BR state_file_max_pages ,
.BR pool_tracked ,
.BR pool_banned ,
.B pool_tracking_limit
and
.BR pool_ban_limit .
The state file cannot grow past 1 GiB; see
.B state_file
in
.BR fail2zig.toml (5).
The tracking pool counts subjects below
.B maxretry
and expired bans not yet retired; the banned count is the subjects with a live ban.
.PP
Paused sources. A file source that cannot resume (truncated in place below its
committed offset, a rotated-out file removed with unread bytes, or a log replaced by a
different file whose previous file is gone, under
.BR "on_truncation = \(dqrefuse\(dq" )
is paused, and the daemon keeps running. When paused sources are the only reason protection
is degraded, the cause is
.B SourcePaused
instead of unknown. Table output adds one
.B Paused:
block per source with the jail, path and cause, then the exact
.B repair-source
command and the reminder that firewall rules for every jail stay in place while the
daemon is stopped. The command is never truncated to the terminal width. When no
offline repair applies to the cause, the block reads that and points to the daemon log.
.PP
Missing sources. A configured file path or glob with no discovered file is shown as
a yellow
.B Missing:
row with its jail and configured path or pattern. This is an availability notice,
not a paused source or repair instruction; the row clears when discovery finds a
matching file. Newer daemon responses add
.B missing_sources
to JSON and plain output; older responses leave this count unknown.
.PP
Loss events. A source that resumed under
.B on_truncation = \(dqrestart\(dq
shows one
.B Loss events:
row with its retained event count and the latest event.
.B --details
adds a section listing every retained continuity-loss event, newest first, up to 256
per source: jail, path, time, reason
.RB ( truncated ,
.B rotated_unread_removed
or
.BR replaced ),
inode, committed offset and observed size. Records written after the committed offset
and removed before they were read were never read; an event records that loss and does
not recover the records.
.PP
New JSON keys, all additive. The existing keys, including
.BR unhealthy_sources ,
keep their meaning.
.IP \(bu 2
.B paused_sources
\(em integer, the sources currently paused.
.IP \(bu 2
.B missing_sources
\(em optional integer, the configured file paths or patterns currently missing; absent
when the daemon response predates this field.
.IP \(bu 2
.B sources
\(em array, always present, empty when none: every source that is paused or has at least one
retained loss event, plus configured missing file paths or patterns, ordered by jail then path. Each element has
.BR jail ,
.B kind
.RB ( file
or
.BR journal ),
.B path
(null for a journal),
.B on_truncation
.RB ( refuse ,
.B restart
or null),
.B state
.RB ( missing ,
.BR paused
or
.BR reading ),
.B cause
(null unless paused),
.BR inode ,
.BR committed_offset ,
.B observed_size
(integers or null),
.B repair
(the exact command, or null when no repair applies),
.B loss_events
(retained count, 0 to 256) and
.B last_loss
(null, or an object with
.BR sequence ,
.BR wall_us ,
.BR reason ,
.BR inode ,
.B committed_offset
and
.BR observed_size ).
.IP \(bu 2
.B cause
gains the value
.BR SourcePaused .
.IP \(bu 2
.B loss_events
\(em only with
.BR --details :
the client adds the array of every retained event, each with
.BR sequence ,
.BR jail ,
.BR path ,
.BR wall_us ,
.BR reason ,
.BR inode ,
.B committed_offset
and
.BR observed_size .
This is the one case where
.B --details
changes JSON output, and it adds a key without changing any other.
.PP
Plain output adds, after every existing key,
.BR paused_sources ,
the optional
.B missing_sources
count when the daemon provides it,
one tab-separated
.B source
line per listed source (jail, kind, path or \-, state, cause or \-, on_truncation or \-,
loss event count, latest loss wall_us or \-, latest loss committed offset or \-), one
.B source_repair
line (jail, path, command) for each source whose repair is non-null, and with
.B --details
one
.B loss_event
line per event. Callers who are not administrators see
.B <redacted>
for a source path and repair command.
.PP
If the events cannot be read after the status was read, table output prints
.BR "Continuity loss events: unavailable (message)" ,
plain output prints
.BR loss_events_unavailable ,
and JSON sets
.B loss_events
to null with
.BR loss_events_unavailable .
The exit status is 3 for a transport failure and 1 when the daemon does not support the
query; upgrade and restart the daemon in that case.
.RE
.TP
.BI stats " [--details] [--output table|plain|json]"
Show one retained enforcement-manager worker snapshot. It reports snapshot age
separately from firewall readback knowledge and age. A stale snapshot remains
labeled with its age; unavailable counters are null in JSON and shown as unavailable
in human output. Counters start at zero when the daemon starts and remain cumulative
across reloads and storage reopen for that daemon process. Accepted readbacks count
accepted inventories, not every dump or readback attempt. This query does not read
SQLite, scan bans, or request a firewall readback. Use
.B status
for protection and health; stats is not a current health assertion. JSON and plain
output include the complete schema regardless of
.BR --details .
.PP
Retry pool counts describe the last completed worker sample. The status fields
.B pool_sample_available
and
.B pool_sample_pending
show whether a sample exists and whether catch-up is pending;
.B pool_sample_as_of_us
is its processing-clock timestamp in microseconds. Before the first sample,
counts are unknown. A pending sample retains the last completed counts.
.TP
.B jails
One row per configured jail: enabled, paused, health, active bans, thresholds,
effective action, configured mode, observed protection, and source. Table output includes the
source failure cause and captured journalctl exit/signal metadata. Raw child stderr
is not displayed. Plain output retains its 11-column layout.
Table output distinguishes MODE (enforce, log-only or unknown) from PROTECTION
(active, unconfirmed, log-only or unknown).
Active means the daemon's existing protection criteria are met; it is not an
independent per-jail kernel verification and does not promise zero pending bans.
Unconfirmed does not mean installed rules are absent. Missing query fields remain
unknown; action names do not determine mode. JSON includes the additive Boolean
\fBenforce_configured\fR while preserving \fBenforcing\fR and \fBaction\fR.
JSON also carries the additive Boolean \fBsource_paused\fR, true when any source of the jail is paused;
\fBsource_healthy\fR and \fBcause\fR keep their meaning.
The additive Boolean \fBsource_missing\fR is true while a source waits for its log file to appear
under \fBon_truncation = "restart"\fR; the source stays healthy and table output shows \fBmissing (FileNotFound)\fR.
Plain output retains its 11 columns and enforcing Boolean position; an unknown ban
count is shown as \fB-\fR, while a known zero remains \fB0\fR. Table output shows state, source health, enforcement and bans; add
.B --details
for thresholds and effective action. When the detailed table does not fit the
terminal width, all detail fields are shown in stacked rows per jail.
.TP
.BI list " [--jail name] [--origin detection|manual|config] [--details] [--sort]"
Active bans, optionally for one jail. Table output distinguishes
confirmed and pending intent, permanent leases and unknown expiry; narrow terminals
use stacked rows. Existing plain columns and JSON fields retain their meaning.
The --details flag adds ban counts to the table view and the retained matched
line of each current live owner decision. JSON adds a
.B matches
array containing the complete retained line or an empty array when unavailable.
Table details escape controls and wrap the complete retained line across
continuation rows at the terminal width. Plain details keep their escaped,
96-byte truncated display; an ellipsis marks truncation.
Log-only decisions, unconfirmed decisions without their own event, expired
configured entries, pruned text and zero retention policy have no matched line.
Shared scopes use the exact owning jail and current decision, without fallback.
Ordinary output is unchanged. The detailed list walk uses 64-item pages and the
same total row and 64 MiB client bounds. Sorted output retains its existing
1 MiB payload bound.
.IP
Every ban has one origin:
.B detection
(a filter matched),
.B manual
.RB ( ban )
or
.B config
.RB ( banip ).
Table output shows it in the
.B ORIGIN
column after
.BR JAIL ,
and each JSON item carries
.B origin
and
.B expired
.RB ( false
for every live ban). After the live bans, each timed
.B banip
entry whose period has run out is listed once as an item with
.B expired
set to
.BR true ,
.B origin
.BR config ,
.B ip
the entry's canonical address,
.BR jail ,
.B expiry_us
and
.B ban_expiry
the stored deadline,
.B permanent
false,
.B ban_count
0, and
.B enforced
and
.B confirmed
false; the table shows
.B expired
under
.BR "TIME LEFT" .
.B \-\-origin
keeps only the bans of that origin, filtered by the client in every output format;
.B \-\-origin config
includes the expired entries.
.IP
.B --sort
sorts returned rows client-side in every output format: IPv4 before IPv6, numeric
address ascending, prefix length ascending, host before an explicit equal-width
network, jail name bytewise, then original row position. IPv4-mapped IPv6 remains
IPv6. It preserves duplicate rows and additive JSON fields. Without the flag, the
existing order is unchanged. Malformed or missing address/jail sort keys fail before
output.
.TP
.BI "firewall show" " [--limit 1..256] [--cursor token] [--details]"
Read the last sampled fail2zig-owned
kernel protection in the daemon's network namespace. Native nftables inspection
does not require the nft executable. The query performs no firewall refresh or mutation.
Observation age and the latest attempt distinguish retained data from a failed
readback. Unavailable, absent and owned-but-empty are separate states; a successful
query exit does not prove installed protection or packet reachability.
With --details, show the tables, chains, sets and attachment rules verified in
the retained observation, plus the location of each sampled entry. This normalized
owned view is not a raw ruleset dump. Structures retained after a failed check are
stale observations, not current-state claims. Structural metadata from older
daemons may be unavailable. JSON adds nullable structure and per-item placement;
--details does not change firewall plain or JSON output.
At most 256 entries are sampled from a complete readback. The default page size is
64. JSON reports observed_total, sample_count and sample_truncated separately from
observation_complete. next_cursor pages only the retained sample; use the same limit.
A replaced observation, daemon restart or 60-second cursor lifetime requires
restarting pagination without a cursor. No jail filter or forced refresh is provided.
Timeouts are sampled values; unexpected entries do not imply desired or confirmed bans.
Each entry carries the origin of the ban that owns it (table column
.BR ORIGIN ,
JSON
.BR origin ),
which is null for an element the daemon does not own.
.TP
.B firewall withdraw
Remove every firewall rule the running daemon realized, in batches with a kernel
readback after each, and hold enforcement off until the next start. Owners and ban
deadlines are kept; new decisions are still recorded but not installed;
.B reload
does not lift the hold. While held,
.B status
reports protection
.B degraded
with cause
.BR FirewallWithdrawn ,
.B jails
reports no jail enforcing, and the daemon logs a warning every hour. A restart
reinstalls every live ban. The outcome is
.B applied
with the number of rules removed (0 when enforcement is already withdrawn),
.B partial
with the count removed and the cause when the turn bound, a stop request or a
transient readback failure interrupts it (run the command again),
.B uncertain
when a kernel failure leaves the remainder unknown, or
.B rejected
when nothing was removed. It works while storage is unhealthy and changes no
stored state, so it has no request replay or audit record. Because a stop leaves
rules in place, run it before stopping the daemon to leave an empty table.
.TP
.BI ban " ip --jail name [--duration seconds|duration] [--scope host|net cidr] [--force]"
Manual ban. Exactly one
.B \-\-jail
is required.
.B \-\-duration
takes seconds or the configuration's duration syntax (2m, 1h30m); without it the
ban runs for the jail's
.BR bantime .
.B \-\-scope net
bans a network and needs a backend with set support (nftables or ipset).
A log-only jail rejects manual bans. The ban has origin
.BR manual .
A
.B ban
of an address that holds a detection ban extends it to the requested duration and
marks it
.BR manual .
A
.B ban
of an address that holds a config ban is refused with exit status 1 and
.BR "banned by config: edit banip"
(JSON
.BR reasons[0] );
edit
.B banip
in the configuration and reload instead.
.IP
Lockout guard. Before it contacts the daemon,
.B ban
refuses a target that is a protected address or a
.B \-\-scope net
network containing one. The protected addresses are loopback (127.0.0.0/8 and ::1),
every IPv4 and IPv6 address of the host's interfaces, and the caller's SSH client
address, the first field of
.BR SSH_CONNECTION .
The refusal exits with status 1, opens no socket and names the matched address:
.PP
.nf
.RS
error: refusing to ban 127.0.0.1: loopback address 127.0.0.1; use --force to override
.RE
.fi
.IP
The reason reads
.BR "loopback address" ,
.B this host's address
or
.BR "your SSH peer address" .
When the interface addresses cannot be read, the guard still protects loopback and
the SSH client address.
.B \-\-force
skips the guard; the daemon's own authorization is unchanged, so the guard is a
convenience against a typing mistake or a faulty script, not a security boundary.
.B \-\-force
on any command other than
.B ban
is a usage error (exit status 2).
.IP
A
.B ban
of an address on the jail's ignore list (configured
.BR ignoreip ,
a runtime entry or a
.B self
address) is applied as before and exits 0, with a warning that names the ignore
source as
.B ignore list
shows it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
warning: 192.0.2.10 is on the ignore list (config)
.RE
.fi
.IP
JSON output carries the same text in the
.B warning
field.
.TP
.BI unban " ip --jail name [--scope host|net cidr]"
Release a manual or detection ban in one jail. A config ban is refused with exit status
1 and
.BR "banned by config: edit banip" .
After an unban the address's failure count starts from zero, so
.B maxretry
new failures inside
.B findtime
ban it again. Its escalation count is kept: that next ban is escalated as the next
offence, not treated as a first one.
.TP
.BI "ignore add" " address|cidr --jail name [--for duration] [--unban]"
Record a runtime exclusion for one jail. The address is an IPv4 or IPv6 host, or
.IR address / prefix
(IPv4 prefix 0\-32, IPv6 prefix 0\-128) of at most 64 bytes, and is stored in canonical form
(host bits below the prefix cleared, RFC 5952 for IPv6). Hostnames are not accepted.
.B \-\-jail
is required and the jail must be configured, enabled or not. A jail with custom rules
.RB ( rule_files )
is refused with exit status 1 and the reason
.BR "jail '<name>' uses rule_files; manage its exclusions through ignore_file" ;
its exclusions stay in
.B ignore_file
plus
.BR "fail2zig reload" .
Flags may come before or after the
address. Entries are stored in the state database, survive restart and reload, and are
merged with the jail's configured
.BR ignoreip .
From the next event the daemon processes after the commit, a matching event creates no
candidate and counts as an ignored detection. An event admitted before the commit may still
finish its ban.
.RS
.PP
An ignore entry does not lift a ban that already exists. When the jail has a ban inside the
entry, the output states it, the deadline (or that it is permanent), that the ban stays, and
the exact command that lifts it. A network ban wider than the entry is reported with its
.B unban \-\-scope net
command.
.B \-\-unban
also lifts that jail's bans inside the entry through the same path as
.BR unban ,
one result line per lifted ban, confirmed, not yet confirmed in the kernel, unknown or
refused. It never lifts a wider network ban, never touches another jail, and lifts at most 256
bans; with more inside the entry the whole command is refused and no entry is recorded.
Adding an entry that already exists as a runtime entry changes nothing, and
.B \-\-unban
still lifts the bans. An entry already in the configuration is recorded as well and its source
becomes
.BR config+runtime .
Output also names any wider configured entry that already covers the address, a prefix 0
entry that covers every address of its family, and a disabled or paused jail.
.PP
.B \-\-for
gives the entry a life, in the duration syntax of
.BR fail2zig.toml (5),
for example
.B \-\-for 2m
or
.BR "\-\-for 1h30m" .
The deadline is stored, survives a restart, and JSON output adds
.BR expires_us .
At the deadline the daemon deletes the entry, and from the next event the address is
detected again. Without
.BR \-\-for ,
the entry takes the jail's
.B ignoretime
when it is set and is permanent otherwise. Adding an existing entry with a later deadline,
or without one, extends it; a permanent entry is never shortened by
.BR \-\-for .
A value that is not a duration, is 0 or is not finite exits with status 2:
.PP
.nf
.RS
invalid \-\-for value '<text>' (expected a duration such as 2m or 1h30m)
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B \-\-for
on an address configured only in
.B ignoreip
exits with status 2 and
.BR "\-\-for applies to runtime entries only; <address> is configured in ignoreip" ;
give the configured entry a duration in the file instead.
.PP
Each jail holds at most 128 entries, configuration and runtime combined. An add that would
exceed it is refused. A reload that would make configured plus runtime entries exceed 128 is
rejected. A daemon start never refuses for that reason: every entry applies and further adds are
refused until the count is 128 or less.
.PP
Table output is text on standard output. Plain output is tab-separated key and value lines (\fBkind\fR, \fBoutcome\fR, \fBenforced\fR, \fBgeneration\fR, \fBmutation_revision\fR,
\fBjail\fR, \fBaddress\fR, \fBentry\fR, \fBsource\fR, \fBcovered_by\fR, \fBbans_total\fR, one
\fBban\fR line per related ban and \fBreason\fR lines). JSON output is the daemon outcome object
unchanged, with the additive keys
.BR entry ,
.BR source ,
.BR covered_by ,
.BR jail_enabled ,
.BR jail_paused ,
.BR unban_requested ,
.B bans_total
and
.BR bans .
In JSON mode standard output holds one JSON document, and daemon outcomes, refusals included, are printed there;
a refusal that is not an outcome object (a request the IPC layer rejects before the handler, such as a monitor
peer's mutation) is printed as an error object on standard error with the same exit class.
.PP
Exit status is the worst of the entry step and every lift, ranked 5 over 4 over 1 over 0:
0 when the entry is added or present and no lift was requested or every lift is confirmed;
4 when a lift is applied but its kernel removal is not yet confirmed;
5 when a lift or the durable commit could not be established;
1 when the entry is refused (unknown jail, 128 bound, more than 256 bans with
.BR \-\-unban )
or a lift is refused; 2 for a usage error such as a missing
.B \-\-jail
or an invalid address or prefix, with nothing sent; 3 when the socket is unavailable.
.RE
.TP
.BI "ignore remove" " address|cidr --jail name"
Remove the jail's runtime entry with that canonical form. It never changes configuration
entries and never touches bans. From the next event after the commit the address is detected
again, with counting from zero, unless the configuration still covers it; in that case the
output says the address remains ignored and names the covering configured entry
(\fBstill_ignored_by\fR in plain and JSON output). An entry set only in the configuration is
refused with the file to edit and
.BR "fail2zig reload" ;
an address with no runtime entry is refused. A jail that is not configured is accepted when
it has stored runtime entries, so they can be cleaned up. A jail with custom rules
.RB ( rule_files )
is refused as for
.BR "ignore add" . Exit status: 0 when removed; 1 for a
configuration-only entry, an absent entry, a custom-rules jail or an unknown jail; 5 when the commit could not be
established; 2 and 3 as for
.BR "ignore add" .
.TP
.BI "ignore list" " [--jail name]"
Read-only. Builtin jails show their effective list. Custom-rule jails show only
configured literals and any self entries in their published custom policy;
ignore_file values are omitted. One row per distinct jail and displayed entry,
ordered by jail name,
then IPv4 before IPv6, numeric address, prefix length. Columns are
.BR JAIL ,
.BR ADDRESS ,
.B SOURCE
and
.BR EXPIRES ,
where the source is
.BR config ,
.BR runtime ,
.B config+runtime
or
.B self
(a host address added by
.BR ignoreself ).
A
.B self
entry never merges with another source: an address that is both a host address and
listed shows twice.
.B EXPIRES
is
.B \-
for a permanent entry, the time left (for example
.BR "in 1m 58s" )
for a timed one, or
.BR expired .
A configured entry and a runtime entry for one address show as
.B config+runtime
only when both are permanent. An expired runtime entry is deleted and no longer listed;
an expired configured entry stays listed as
.B expired
until it is removed from the file. JSON entries add
.B expires_us
(null when permanent),
.B remaining_s
(null when permanent, 0 once expired) and
.BR expired .
Without
.B \-\-jail
every enabled jail is listed; with it, that jail whether enabled or not. A jail without
entries prints no rows and exits 0; an unknown jail exits 1. Table output ends with a
.B Total:
line and notes for a custom-rule jail's
.B ignore_file
(its configured literals are listed with source
.BR config ;
the file's entries are not). This custom projection is not the complete exclusion
policy. A disabled custom jail queried explicitly shows its configured literals,
without runtime or invented self entries. A removed custom jail has no current
configured-prefix view; an explicit query shows only actual stored runtime rows,
or reports an unknown jail when none remain. Other notes cover runtime entries
stored for jails that are not
configured, and for a jail above the 128 limit after a configuration change. Plain output is
one tab-separated line per row without header or footer. JSON output carries
.BR limit ,
.BR redacted ,
per-jail
.B entries
and an
.B unconfigured
array. The top-level limit remains the builtin 128-entry limit. A custom jail
adds its own
.B limit
of 1024, which covers the combined custom policy, including omitted file/self
values. Displayed counts are not remaining admission slots. Custom entries do
not acquire builtin timed or runtime provenance; rule_files jails reject those
mutations and timed settings. Members of the
.B fail2zig
group may run it; for them every address and
.B ignore_file
is printed as
.BR <redacted> ,
as
.B config
does for address lists.
.PP
Both mutating subcommands return exit status 1 with
.B mutation requires root or daemon uid (code 403)
on standard error for a group member, and
.B daemon does not support ignore commands
(code 400) when the daemon predates them; upgrade and restart it. A mutation that
raced a reload or other change is rejected with the daemon's reason and should be retried.
When storage is not accepting changes the command is rejected until
.B status
shows storage healthy.
.TP
.B reload
Propose the current configuration file as a new generation. Outcomes:
.BR noop ", " applied ", " rejected " or " restart_required
(the restart-only keys are listed in
.BR reasons ).
Live keys: per-jail
.BR maxretry ", " bantime ", " bantime_kind ", " bantime_increment ,
.BR bantime_increment_decay ,
literal
.B ignoreip
and
.B banip
entries and their durations,
.B ignoreself
and
.B ignoretime
(per jail and in
.BR [defaults] ),
global
.BR log_level ,
and the contents of a custom jail's
.BR ignore_file .
An
.B ignoreip
edit applies from the next event after the reload and never alters an existing ban; a
.B banip
edit bans or lifts at once. The reload reads the host's addresses again for
.BR ignoreself .
.B log_format
is restart-only.
Live reload applies supported changes; restart-only settings require a daemon restart.
.IP
Adding or removing a jail is a live change. An added enabled jail starts in the same
reload. A removed jail is withdrawn last, after every other change has committed: its
bans are lifted, its elements are removed from the shared kernel sets, its retry and source
state is deleted, and its history is kept, so
.B history \-\-jail
.I name
still shows its bans. The daemon logs one line per removed jail:
.PP
.nf
.RS
native: jail=<name> removed: <n> bans lifted
.RE
.fi
.IP
A start whose configuration no longer names a jail that holds state retires that jail the
same way. If any jail of the proposed configuration fails validation, the whole reload is
.B rejected
with a reason naming that jail, and every running jail is unchanged.
.TP
.BI history " [--jail name] [--limit 1..256] [--cursor token] [--details]"
Page through confirmed ban history;
.B next_cursor
continues a listing. Each event carries the origin of its ban
.RB ( detection ", " manual " or " config ;
JSON
.BR origin ),
and every origin change is kept as an event. Events written before 0.4.8 show
.BR detection .
With --details, include the stored matched line of each confirmed event.
JSON adds a zero-or-one-element
.B matches
array with the complete retained line. Table and plain output escape untrusted
controls. Table details wrap the complete retained line across continuation
rows at the terminal width. Plain details truncate display to 96 bytes.
Missing, pruned or zero-policy evidence is absent; confirmation facts remain.
Detail queries require administrator access through the local socket and are
not exposed by HTTP. The default page is 64 events. Explicit larger pages remain
subject to the complete 1 MiB response limit; reduce --limit after a 413 response.
Without --details, existing fields and output are unchanged.
.TP
.BI "history reset " "ip (--jail name | --all)"
Reset the durable history for one address.
.TP
.BI "jail enable|disable|pause|resume " name
Administer one jail. Disabling releases its effects; pausing keeps them. An unknown
jail action lists valid actions and points to
.B list --jail name
to inspect jail bans,
.B ban ip --jail name
to add one, and
.B unban ip --jail name
to release one.
.PP
Disable releases owners in bounded worker turns. Small operations wait up to two
seconds for coherent kernel confirmation. A partial or uncertain result continues
automatically while the jail remains disabled, including after restart; inspect
.B jails
and
.B status
instead of repeatedly issuing disable. Jail JSON includes
.BR disable_cleanup_pending ,
which describes current cleanup independently of a historical request outcome.
An old applied request is not rewritten when remaining cleanup is discovered.
Shared scopes remain installed when another jail still owns them.
.PP
Enable cancels remaining disable work; already committed releases remain released.
Pause and resume do not enable a disabled jail. Automatic detection and configured
ban entries cannot create owners while disabled. A manual ban requires enabling
the jail first; migration activation refuses disabled destination jails atomically.
Unban remains available. Removing a jail transfers remaining cleanup to retirement.
.TP
.B config
The effective configuration and its generation. Paths and address lists are
redacted unless the caller is an administrator.
.PP
Use
.B enforce = true|false
in defaults or a jail to configure protection intent. The legacy
.B banaction
input remains accepted as a deprecated compatibility setting; it is not the
recommended spelling. The global
.B firewall
setting selects the backend. Configured intent and observed protection are separate.
.TP
.B version
Daemon version over the socket. It contacts the daemon, unlike
.BR \-\-version .
Table output combines matching client and daemon versions on one
line and labels each separately when they differ. Plain and JSON output retain
separate client and daemon fields.
.TP
.BI completions " bash|zsh|fish"
Shell completion script on stdout.
.TP
.BI help " [command]"
General or per-command help;
.I command
.B \-\-help
prints the same page. An unknown topic exits 2.
.
.SH RULE-TEST
.B fail2zig rule-test
evaluates one input against one builtin service or one native rule file with the
daemon's own decoder, time admission and detectors. It opens no state store, no
firewall and no daemon socket.
.TP
Input (exactly one)
.BI \-\-file " path"
(regular file, streamed, at most 64 MiB, lines up to 64 KiB),
.BI \-\-record " line" ,
or
.B \-\-journal
[\fImatch\fR...] through the fixed
.BR journalctl (1)
invocation.
.TP
Rule (exactly one)
.BI \-\-service " name"
(one of the compiled services) or
.BI \-\-rule\-file " path"
(bounded native JSON rule, at most 4096 bytes).
.TP
Options
.BI \-\-config " path"
[\fB\-\-jail\fR \fIname\fR] resolves timestamp, time zone and ignore settings from a
native configuration;
.BI \-\-timestamp " iso8601|syslog|epoch_seconds|common_log|undated" ,
.BI \-\-time\-zone " id" ,
.BI \-\-tz\-offset " minutes" ,
.BI \-\-year " yyyy" ,
.BI \-\-encoding " utf8|ascii|latin1|utf16le|utf16be|utf32le|utf32be" ,
.BI \-\-identity " raw|dns"
(\fBdns\fR resolves hostnames only through
.BI \-\-dns\-server " ip:port"
with a bounded timeout; the default never touches the network),
.BI \-\-ignore " cidr" ,
.BI \-\-max\-lines " n" ,
.BI \-\-limit " n"
(samples, default 20),
.BI \-\-output " json|table" ,
.BR \-\-print\-lines ,
.BI \-\-now " epoch-seconds" ,
.BI \-\-window " seconds" .
.PP
Samples report the outcome
.RB ( match ", " miss ", " ignore ", " reject ),
the reason, the extracted identity, the event time and the time disposition.
Raw lines are included only with
.BR \-\-print\-lines .
Exit 0 when the input was processed (even with zero matches), 1 when the rule,
configuration or input was refused, 2 on usage errors.
.
.SH MIGRATE
Read-only preparation:
.B inspect
(\fB\-\-source\-dir\fR),
.B snapshot
(\fB\-\-source\-db\fR \fB\-\-staging\-dir\fR),
.B plan
(\fB\-\-source\-dir\fR, optional \fB\-\-source\-db\fR with \fB\-\-staging\-dir\fR,
\fB\-\-out\fR, \fB\-\-continuity lossless|reset-replay\fR, \fB\-\-replay\-window\fR,
\fB\-\-runtime\-socket\fR)
and
.B validate
(\fB\-\-plan\fR). The staging directory must be private (0700) and non-volatile.
.PP
.B cutover
(\fB\-\-plan \-\-state\-file \-\-staging\-dir \-\-backend nftables|ipset|iptables\fR
[\fB\-\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-run\-id\fR]) stages the destination offline with the daemon
stopped and drives activation and kernel verification inside the daemon. It is
journaled: an unreachable socket exits 3 and prints the exact
.B \-\-run\-id
command to resume, and a rerun classifies the journal instead of replaying.
.B status
reports a run.
.PP
.B rollback
(\fB\-\-plan \-\-state\-file \-\-staging\-dir \-\-backend \-\-run\-id\fR
[\fB\-\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-source\-verified\fR]) reverses an activated run in two
journaled phases. First it validates the staging recovery point against the plan's
snapshot and fingerprints the live source (WAL-aware); a mismatch is
.B rollback_failed
(exit 4) with nothing written, an environment failure is
.B operational_failure
(exit 1) and resumable. It then carries the post-cutover deltas back, moves the original
with its journal siblings to
.IR <source>.pre-rollback-<run16> ,
renames the fsync'd derived database into place, records a
.I restored-<run16>
marker in the staging directory and exits 3 with the next command. Then, with
.BR \-\-source\-verified ,
the source control socket present and a running
.B fail2ban-server
process, it releases the migrated scopes from the daemon: exit 0 with state
.BR rolled_back ;
partial (4), uncertain (5) or refused (1) releases leave the step open; an unreachable
daemon exits 3. See
.IR docs/operations/command-migration.md .
.
.SH REPAIR-SOURCE
A file source truncated in place below its committed position (for example a
PortSentry history file that the scanner empties on start), a rotated-out file
removed while it still held unread bytes, or a log replaced by a different file whose
previous file exists nowhere in its directory, is not silently re-read under the default
.BR "on_truncation = \(dqrefuse\(dq" .
The daemon starts with that source paused: the startup journal line and
.B fail2zig status
name the jail and source and print the exact command to run.
.B repair-source
acknowledges the loss for one configured file source.
.PP
The command first tries the daemon's administration socket. When the daemon answers, it
performs the repair inside its running worker, with the same token, the same refusals and
the same durable outcome as the offline form; the source leaves
.B paused
and resumes, and every jail's firewall rules stay installed throughout. The online form is
a mutation, so it needs uid 0 or the daemon uid, and it writes an audit line. When the
socket is absent or refuses the connection, the command runs the offline form on the
stopped daemon's state. Both forms print the same text and use the same exit status.
.PP
The repair works the same way in both forms. After a truncation in place it
restarts the source at offset 0 of the current file. After a removed rotated file it
records the loss of that file's unread bytes and resumes the current file at its
committed offset, not at 0. After a replaced log it records the loss of the previous
file's unread bytes and restarts the source at offset 0 of the file now at the path.
The offline form holds the state lock. The repair never creates state, and
refuses a source that is not truncated in place, not replaced with its previous file
gone, and not missing a removed rotated incarnation (nothing to acknowledge: an absent
path, a rewrite in place, or a previous file still in its directory), a pending receipt for that source, consumer
state that is not ready, or a checkpoint changed since observation. Other sources,
owners and ban deadlines are not changed.
.PP
If the source is not paused because
.B on_truncation = \(dqrestart\(dq
already recorded the loss, the command prints that event, performs no repair, does not
reserve the token and exits 0.
.PP
The
.I token
(1\(en128 of A\(enZ a\(enz 0\(en9 . _ : \-) identifies the repair. The daemon proposes one
of the form
.IR ack-<inode>-<committed_offset>-<detected_unix_seconds> ,
so each incident has its own token and the printed command can be copied as it is.
Repeating the same command replays the committed outcome without a second repair; a
different token after a successful repair is refused, because the source is no longer
truncated. The newest 256
outcomes are retained; an older token stays reserved and is refused. Data written
after the committed position and removed by the truncation was never read and cannot
be recovered (for a removed rotated file or a replaced log, the records after the
committed offset of the file that is gone), and lines still in the file are read again. A
daemon stopped for the offline form leaves its firewall rules for every jail in place;
the next start reconciles them with durable state. Exit 0 on success or replay, 1 on
refusal, 2 on usage errors.
.
.SH SUPPORT-BUNDLE
.B support-bundle
writes one JSON file to attach to a bug report. Run it as the daemon's user:
it reads the configuration, opens the state file read-only and asks the daemon over
its socket. A section it cannot collect records
.B {\(dqerror\(dq: ...}
and the bundle is still written, so it also works while the daemon is stopped.
.PP
Contents: the client version, target and build mode; the daemon version,
.BR status ,
.B jails
and
.B stats
payloads; every retained continuity loss event; the effective configuration from the
daemon, the SHA-256 of the configuration file and the digest, generation and commit time
the daemon last loaded, with whether they match; the state schema, page size, page count,
freelist pages, and each table's row count and bytes (indexes counted with their table);
the kernel firewall observation without its elements, and the sampled element count per
address family; the configured memory and descriptor ceilings with the daemon process's
.BR VmRSS ,
.BR VmHWM ,
.B VmSize
and thread count; the kernel release, machine, CPU count, total and available memory and
.BR "nft \-\-version" ;
and the last
.I n
warning and error lines of the daemon log (default 200, at most 2000; 0 skips it). The log
is the
.B log_target
file, or the
.B fail2zig.service
journal at priority warning and above when
.B log_target
is
.BR stderr .
.PP
Redaction applies to every string in the bundle. Each IPv4 or IPv6 address, with an
optional
.BI / prefix
or IPv4
.BI : port
suffix, becomes
.BI v4\- hash
or
.BI v6\- hash
(the first 48 bits of an HMAC-SHA-256 under a key drawn for this bundle and never
written), so one address keeps one label inside a bundle and cannot be matched across bundles. Loopback and
unspecified addresses are kept.
.B \-\-include\-addresses
keeps every address. Any path other than a configured
.B logpath
or one of its expansions becomes
.BR <path> .
The bundle carries no ban list.
.PP
The default output is
.IR ./fail2zig\-support\-YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ.json ,
created mode 0600 and never overwritten; the path is printed on stdout.
.B \-\-out \-
writes the bundle to stdout. Each incomplete section is named on stderr. Exit 0 when
every section was collected, 4 when the bundle was written with sections that record an
error, 1 when nothing was written, 2 on usage errors.
.
.SH METRICS
The HTTP listener's
.I /metrics
page (see
.B metrics_enabled
in
.BR fail2zig.toml (5))
adds these series to the existing ones, which keep their names. Per enabled jail, with
the label
.BI jail=\(dq name \(dq :
.TP
.B fail2zig_jail_bans_active
Gauge: live bans.
.TP
.B fail2zig_jail_bans_total
Counter: bans.
.TP
.B fail2zig_jail_failures_counted_total
Counter: failures counted toward a ban.
.TP
.B fail2zig_jail_ignored_detections_total
Counter: detections skipped because the address is on the ignore list.
.TP
.B fail2zig_jail_source_state
Gauge: 0 ok, 1 paused, 2 missing, 3 blocked.
.TP
.B fail2zig_jail_pool_evictions_total
Counter: idle subjects evicted from the tracking pool since the daemon started.
.PP
Daemon-wide gauges:
.BR fail2zig_state_file_bytes ,
.BR fail2zig_state_file_pages ,
.BR fail2zig_pool_tracked ,
.BR fail2zig_pool_banned ,
.B fail2zig_pool_tracking_limit
and
.BR fail2zig_pool_ban_limit .
A jail's series appear when it starts or is added by a reload and stop when it is
removed.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Every command returns one of the frozen classes.
.TP
.B 0
Success; every claimed effect was observed.
.TP
.B 1
Valid request rejected by the daemon or the local operation (fail-closed startup,
log-only jail, zero jails imported, refused rule or input).
.TP
.B 2
Usage, argument, configuration parse or validation failure.
.TP
.B 3
Daemon or transport unavailable (no socket, refused, timeout).
.TP
.B 4
A durable effect was applied but kernel confirmation is incomplete.
.TP
.B 5
A durable effect may have been applied and could not be established.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/fail2zig/config.toml
Configuration; see
.BR fail2zig.toml (5).
.TP
.I /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock
Administration socket, mode 0660, group
.BR fail2zig .
The parent directory must be owned by root or the daemon uid with no group or
other write bit; the daemon refuses to serve otherwise.
.TP
.I /var/lib/fail2zig/state.bin
Durable SQLite state: records, decisions, effects, history and source positions.
The parent and database must be owned by the daemon UID, with no group or
other write access. This is not the legacy v0.3.0 binary state format.
.TP
.I global.log_target
Optional append-only log file (mode 0640) instead of stderr; reopened on
.BR SIGUSR1 .
.
.SH SERVICE ACCOUNT AND UPGRADE
The shipped unit runs as the non-login user and group
.BR fail2zig ,
retaining CAP_NET_ADMIN, CAP_NET_RAW and CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH for firewall and log access.
Root or the daemon UID may mutate protection; service-group membership grants
socket monitoring only. CAP_NET_RAW is required by the iptables ipset extension
and also permits raw IP sockets. HTTP runs in the same process with the same capabilities.
.PP
Stop every daemon and state writer and preserve configuration, executable and
coherent state backups before upgrading. The installer does not enable, start
or restart services. It changes ownership only for the default native SQLite
directory, database and existing WAL/SHM files; active writers, unsafe paths,
legacy binary state and automatic custom-path upgrades are refused.
.PP
No automatic conversion of v0.3.0 binary state is provided. Starting a fresh
native database does not preserve its saved bans, counters, history or source
positions. Supported fail2ban schema-4 SQLite migration is a separate workflow.
Native destination migration must run as the service UID with the required
capabilities and caller-owned private staging, with the destination daemon
stopped. See docs/operations/command-migration.md and migration-continuity.md.
.PP
v0.4.7 is the minimum supported upgrade source. The first start of this version
upgrades a state last written by v0.4.8 (schema 26) to schema 28, which adds the
records that let a removed or disabled jail's state be cleaned in bounded steps. A
state last written by v0.4.7 (schema 25) first takes the v0.4.8 step to schema 26.
States last written by v0.4.6 or earlier are not a supported upgrade path; upgrade
them through v0.4.7 first, or start from a fresh state. Before each change the daemon
writes a coherent copy of the database to a backup, which needs free space of twice
the database size plus 32 MiB, and refuses to start if that file already exists; move
an old backup aside only when it is not the sole copy of earlier state.
.IP \(bu 2
The backup before the schema 28 step is
.IR <state_file>.pre27-backup ;
a v0.4.7 state also gets
.IR <state_file>.pre26-backup .
.PP
Each step commits in one transaction, so an interrupted upgrade leaves the state at
the schema it last committed. v0.4.8 and older binaries refuse schema 28 state with their
.B UnsupportedSchema
message. Keep the backup until the upgraded daemon has been checked. Rollback means stopping the daemon, restoring the
backup the upgrade wrote over the state file, removing the upgraded database's
WAL/SHM files, and reinstalling the binary that last wrote that state.
.PP
The first start on state whose saved detection generations hashed the ignore list
or the per-jail subject share (since replaced by one shared capacity) rekeys them once, before admission validation, and logs
.BR "saved detection generations rekeyed without the ignore list or the subject share" .
No ban, deadline, checkpoint or retry subject changes, and later starts write nothing.
A configuration that still differs from saved state in another way keeps its existing
.B RetryGenerationMismatch
refusal.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by
.BR systemd (1)
for
.BR Type=notify ;
absent means notifications are disabled.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for
.B ipset
and
.B iptables
when that backend is selected.
.B journalctl
is invoked at
.I /usr/bin/journalctl
only.
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION
Read by
.B ban
only: its first field is the caller's SSH client address, which the lockout guard
protects.
.PP
No other variables are read by the daemon or the administration commands.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo fail2zig --validate-config --config /etc/fail2zig/config.toml
sudo fail2zig status
fail2zig stats --output plain
fail2zig list --sort --jail sshd
fail2zig list --jail sshd --output json
sudo fail2zig ban 192.0.2.10 --jail sshd --duration 3600
fail2zig list --origin config --output json
sudo fail2zig ignore add 198.51.100.20 --jail sshd
sudo fail2zig ignore add 198.51.100.21 --jail sshd --for 2h
fail2zig ignore list --jail sshd
sudo fail2zig reload
fail2zig rule-test --file /var/log/auth.log --service sshd --tz-offset 0
fail2zig completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/fail2zig
.fi
.
